\subsection{Custom parameters}{

Argument \code{pathwaydata_params} controls additional options:
\itemize{
\item \code{refresh_genes=TRUE} enables \code{genejam::freshenGenes()} which
uses Bioconductor annotations to update the current gene annotation
to the current version available (to the active R session).
Several arguments are passed to this function:
\itemize{
\item \code{ann_lib}: the core annotation library,
'org.Hs.eg.db' is default for Human; 'org.Mm.eg.db' is used
for Mouse. This annotation database must be available,
for example install Rat data with
\code{BiocManager::install('org.Rn.eg.db')}.
Alternatively, \code{ann_lib} can be a \code{list} with one or more
\code{AnnotationDbi} or \code{OrgDb} objects.
\item \code{handle_multiple='best_each'}: converts each gene to the best
available match, using the priority order defined in \code{try_list}.
\item \code{ignore.case=TRUE}: Note that Human symbols are UPPERCASE,
so for a moderate speed boost use \code{ignore.case=FALSE}.
Note that Mouse by convention uses mixed case, for example "Apoe",
and so for mouse and most other species, use \code{ignore.case=TRUE}.
\item \code{empty_rule='empty'}: converts any unmatched entry to '' or NA
so it is not retained in the output. This decision prevents keeping
a gene or accession which can never be recognized as an official
gene during pathway enrichment analysis.
Use \code{empty_rule='original'} to retain the original entry whenever
the entry cannot be matched to any other annotation in \code{try_list}.
\item \code{try_list=c('SYMBOL2EG', 'ACCNUM2EG', 'ENSEMBL2EG', 'ALIAS2EG')}:
is the recommended order of sources to use when equating a gene
symbol or identifier with Bioconductor gene annotations. They are
attempted in order, to convert each entry to an ENTREZID ('EG').
\itemize{
\item When using \code{handle_multiple='best_each'}, each method in \code{try_list}
is attempted, in order, for each gene entry, and the first
successful result is retained as one or more \code{ENTREZID} values
for each input gene.
\item Specifically, when \code{'SYMBOL2EG'} is successful, it is used.
Only when \code{'SYMBOL2EG'} is not successful, \code{'ACCNUM2EG'} is
attempted. Only when \code{'ACCNUM2EG'} is also unsuccessful,
\code{'ENSEMBL2EG'} is used, and so on.
\item The lowest priority is \code{'ALIAS2EG'} since this association has
the lowest confidence for association, and is most prone to
multiple associations. In general pathway enrichment is somewhat
tolerant to such lower-confidence associations, in return for
having more potential opportunities for the correct association.
\item Finally, the matching ENTREZID values are converted to 'SYMBOL'
so the "official gene SYMBOL" is associated with the original
gene entry.
\item For each pathway, only the unique \code{'SYMBOL'} values are retained.
\item In the event two gene identifiers in a pathway are converted to
the same \code{'SYMBOL'}, only the unique \code{'SYMBOL'} is associated with
the corresponding pathways.
\item In the event one gene identifier is converted to two 'SYMBOL' values
of equivalent priority, both 'SYMBOL' values are retained and
associated with the corresponding pathways.
